    "alt": "Cleaning tools, camera, tailoring supplies, laptop, plant-care kit and shipping parcel representing six business models.",
    "html": "<p><strong>A useful small-business idea fits a specific customer problem, a deliverable offer and the cash and hours you can commit. Screen possible models against real demand and operating constraints before choosing one from a list.</strong></p><p>All business names, amounts and operating results in the worked examples are fictional teaching assumptions in USD. They are not industry averages, actual customer results or forecasts for your business.</p><h2 id=\"shortlist\">Compare models by what must be true</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Six starting points to investigate, not earnings recommendations</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Model</th><th scope=\"col\">Customer problem</th><th scope=\"col\">Main constraint</th><th scope=\"col\">First evidence to collect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Recurring home cleaning</th><td>Reliable scheduled cleaning</td><td>Travel, crew time and rework</td><td>Local paid trial and repeat booking</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Bookkeeping support</th><td>Timely organized business records</td><td>Competence, access and scope</td><td>Owner interviews and defined pilot</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Product photography</th><td>Usable catalog images</td><td>Editing time and revisions</td><td>Paid sample with acceptance criteria</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Plant-care visits</th><td>Maintained office plants</td><td>Route density and replacement costs</td><td>Nearby-site trial and travel log</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Made-to-order alterations</th><td>Clothing fit or repair</td><td>Skilled production hours</td><td>Paid job with measured completion time</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Specialty resale</th><td>Hard-to-find selected products</td><td>Inventory, returns and cash cycle</td><td>Small-batch sell-through and net receipts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These models have different operating burdens. A home service can avoid inventory while still needing transport, insurance and practical schedule capacity. A digital service can avoid a storefront while creating customer-acquisition, software and revision costs. A product business can show sales growth while cash remains tied up in unsold stock.</p><h2 id=\"screen\">Use disqualifying constraints before scoring enthusiasm</h2><p>Write your available cash, weekly hours, required personal income, relevant skills, geographic reach and unacceptable commitments. Exclude ideas that cannot meet those constraints under a plausible downside case. For regulated or sensitive work, verify the actual professional, insurance and local requirements before accepting customers. A broad idea list cannot establish permission to practice.</p><p>Then compare demand evidence, contribution per constrained hour, startup commitments, time to collection and repeatability. Keep unknowns visible instead of assigning a precise score to an unsupported guess. A signed paid pilot is stronger evidence than compliments from friends, but a single pilot still does not prove recurring demand.</p><h2 id=\"numbers\">Compare two fictional pilots on the same cost boundary</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Original pilot economics; both cases exclude general overhead and income tax</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Measure</th><th scope=\"col\">Cleaning visit</th><th scope=\"col\">Photography package</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Price</th><td>$180</td><td>$450</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Relevant cash delivery costs</th><td>$55</td><td>$90</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Contribution before owner time</th><td>$125</td><td>$360</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Total owner-hours including travel/admin</th><td>4</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Contribution per owner-hour</th><td>$31.25</td><td>$30.00</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Pilot startup purchases</th><td>$600</td><td>$1,800</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The photography package has a larger ticket, but $360 divided by 12 hours is $30 per hour; cleaning yields $125 divided by four, or $31.25. Neither figure is take-home pay. They must still cover the included owner-income requirement, fixed costs, idle time and any omitted commitments. If the cleaning visit needs five hours instead of four, contribution per hour falls to $25.</p><p>Suppose a month offers 80 practical owner-hours and 20 hours are needed for prospecting and general administration outside those job totals. The remaining 60 hours support at most 15 four-hour visits or five twelve-hour photography packages. Customer demand might be below either ceiling. Avoid multiplying a full calendar by a price before checking all the hours.</p><h2 id=\"test\">Design a small test with a stop rule</h2><p>Choose one customer segment and one tightly specified offer. Ask how the customer currently solves the problem, what causes dissatisfaction and how buying decisions are made. Compare actual alternatives and test a paid delivery at the intended price where practical. Record acquisition effort, delivery time, changes, refunds and collection dates.</p><p>Before the test, set a spending ceiling and a decision rule. For example: test three paid jobs, keep total test cash below a chosen $400 limit and investigate any job that needs over five hours. Those are fictional experiment settings, not universal validation thresholds. If the test misses the rule, revise scope or stop; do not redefine success after seeing the result.</p><h2 id=\"choose\">Turn the best-supported model into a funding plan</h2><p>Obtain dated supplier quotes and identify one-time, recurring and refundable amounts. Include operating shortfalls before customers pay, a separately chosen reserve and the owner’s own living needs outside the business budget. Avoid adding the same startup payment to both setup spending and the cash-gap total.</p><p>The <a href=\"/calculators/business-startup-cost-calculator/\">startup cost calculator</a> helps organize cash needs; the <a href=\"/resources/business-plan-template/\">business plan template</a> records the chosen model. When you need a demand schedule, use the <a href=\"/blogs/sales-forecast-small-business/\">small-business sales forecast</a>. Choose the idea because the evidence and constraints fit, then preserve the assumptions so the first month’s actual results can challenge them.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Download the editable decision record</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/small-business-ideas-worksheet.html\" download>Download the printable HTML worksheet</a>. It includes the completed case and blank fields for your evidence, assumptions, dates and decision. It does not calculate automatically. Edits stay in the open browser until you print or save your completed copy; keep your original separately.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and method</h2><p>The linked sources support the general framework. The calculations, operating constraints and decision examples here are original MyBreakEven teaching material.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.sba.gov/counseling/plan-your-business/\">SBA: business planning and market research</a></li></ul>",
