    "html": "<p><strong>Start a small business by confirming a customer problem, testing a specific offer, estimating delivery economics and dated cash needs, then completing the requirements for your location and opening with reliable records. Review actual results before increasing commitments.</strong></p><p>All business names, amounts and operating results in the worked examples are fictional teaching assumptions in USD. They are not industry averages, actual customer results or forecasts for your business.</p><h2 id=\"evidence\">Define the first customer and the smallest useful offer</h2><p>Describe who buys, the outcome they want, the alternative they use and the scope you can deliver consistently. Interview likely buyers and compare local alternatives. A large market estimate is not a list of customers who will buy from you. Keep confirmed orders, customer interest and assumptions in separate categories.</p><p>Test price and scope together. Record the time and cash required to deliver a paid pilot, including revisions, travel, payment fees and customer support. Choose a spending ceiling and a date to decide whether to continue. Use the <a href=\"/blogs/small-business-ideas/\">business-idea screening guide</a> when you are still choosing between models.</p><h2 id=\"budget\">Reconcile setup spending and early operating cash</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Fictional launch plan for a small service business</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Cash requirement</th><th scope=\"col\">Amount</th><th scope=\"col\">Boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Tools and setup purchases</th><td>$2,400</td><td>Paid before opening</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Security deposit</th><td>$600</td><td>Cash paid; not assumed expense</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">First two months operating shortfall</th><td>$2,000</td><td>Receipts less payments after setup</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Chosen cash cushion</th><td>$1,500</td><td>Separate management assumption</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Total initial funding need</th><td>$6,500</td><td>No setup amounts counted twice</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Owner cash committed</th><td>$4,500</td><td>Confirmed available source</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Unfunded difference</th><td>$2,000</td><td>Resolve before commitments</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The funding need is $2,400 + $600 + $2,000 + $1,500 = $6,500. Subtracting $4,500 confirmed owner cash leaves $2,000 unresolved. That difference is not automatically an approved loan. Reduce or stage commitments, obtain documented funding or postpone launch until the dated schedule works. Include any financing fees, interest and repayments when a specific option is assessed.</p><p>The $2,000 shortfall means operating cash outflows exceed receipts by that amount during the two-month schedule, after excluding the separately listed setup payments. If your forecast already contains those setup payments, do not add them a second time. Budget household living costs separately and confirm what the owner must withdraw from the business.</p><h2 id=\"economics\">Check whether the offer can cover the monthly requirement</h2><p>Suppose one service sells for $250 and its defined job-linked costs are $100. Contribution is $150 per completed job. Covering $1,200 monthly business overhead requires eight jobs before owner compensation, interest and tax under that cost boundary. Adding a separately chosen $2,400 owner-income planning requirement raises the combined target to 24 jobs: ($1,200 + $2,400) ÷ $150.</p><p>If delivery takes four hours per job, 24 jobs require 96 job-hours, plus selling and administration outside the quoted job time. Check your practical calendar and expected demand. A personal income target is not necessarily a deductible accounting expense; label it as a planning requirement. The <a href=\"/calculators/business-startup-cost-calculator/\">startup calculator</a> helps organize launch assumptions without replacing that distinction.</p><h2 id=\"setup\">Complete the requirements for the actual business</h2><p>Use the SBA’s launch guidance as a starting point to identify questions about structure, name registration, tax identifiers, banking, permits and insurance. Requirements depend on activity, state and locality. Verify current obligations with the relevant government agencies and advisers before trading; there is no single checklist that grants permission for every US business.</p><p>Keep business transactions identifiable from the start. Establish invoice numbering, supplier records, receipt storage, expense categories and bank reconciliation. Confirm who maintains the books and how payroll or customer deposits will be handled if relevant. Do not choose a structure solely from a generic cost comparison without considering the actual ownership and obligations.</p><h2 id=\"launch\">Launch with a short review cycle and a visible cash cutoff</h2><ol><li>Confirm the offer, price, delivery scope and customer acceptance process.</li><li>Resolve the funding schedule and distinguish committed funds from applications.</li><li>Check required registrations and operational permissions for the real location.</li><li>Record the first invoices, receipts, delivery costs and work hours.</li><li>Compare the first month with the original assumptions and approve the next commitments.</li></ol><p>Use the <a href=\"/resources/business-plan-template/\">business plan template</a> to connect the evidence and funding decision. Use the <a href=\"/calculators/cash-flow-forecast-template/\">13-week cash forecast</a> for payment dates and <a href=\"/blogs/rolling-forecast/\">rolling forecasting</a> for future operating assumptions. Preserve the original forecast so later changes remain explainable.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Download the editable decision record</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/how-to-start-small-business-worksheet.html\" download>Download the printable HTML worksheet</a>. It includes the completed case and blank fields for your evidence, assumptions, dates and decision. It does not calculate automatically. Edits stay in the open browser until you print or save your completed copy; keep your original separately.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and method</h2><p>The linked sources support the general framework. The calculations, operating constraints and decision examples here are original MyBreakEven teaching material.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.sba.gov/counseling/plan-your-business/\">SBA: plan your business</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.sba.gov/counseling/launch-your-business/\">SBA: launch requirements and setup</a></li></ul>",
