    "id": "N014",
    "slug": "business-proposal-template",
    "title": "Business Proposal Template: Scope, Price and an Acceptable Delivery",
    "seoTitle": "Business Proposal Template: Scope and Pricing",
    "description": "Use an editable client proposal with clear deliverables, exclusions, pricing assumptions, payment milestones and acceptance fields. Check the cost case first.",
    "metaDescription": "Use an editable client proposal with clear deliverables, exclusions, pricing assumptions, payment milestones and acceptance fields. Check the cost case first.",
    "primaryKeyword": "business proposal template",
    "scopeBoundary": "Defined client engagement; business plan and internal investment proposal remain different tasks.",
    "opening": "A business proposal explains a specific client problem, the deliverables you will provide, the schedule, price, assumptions and process for acceptance. Build the delivery cost case before promising the scope, then turn the agreed details into the appropriate written engagement.",
    "tag": "Business Planning",
    "tags": [
      "Business finance",
      "Planning",
      "Worked example"
    ],
    "cluster": "concepts",
    "prepared": "2026-11-04",
    "editorialStatus": "draft",
    "image": "/images/title-covers/day2-business-proposal-template-20261104.webp",
    "alt": "Client and provider reviewing a written proposal, scope checklist and delivery timeline.",
    "html": "<p><strong>A business proposal explains a specific client problem, the deliverables you will provide, the schedule, price, assumptions and process for acceptance. Build the delivery cost case before promising the scope, then turn the agreed details into the appropriate written engagement.</strong></p><p>All business names, amounts and operating results in the worked examples are fictional teaching assumptions in USD. They are not industry averages, actual customer results or forecasts for your business.</p><h2 id=\"structure\">Use a proposal the client can evaluate</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Editable proposal sections and the question each answers</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Section</th><th scope=\"col\">What to write</th><th scope=\"col\">Review control</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Client objective</th><td>Specific problem and agreed success evidence</td><td>Confirm the buyer recognizes it</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Deliverables</th><td>Named outputs, quantities and formats</td><td>Define completion for each output</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Scope and exclusions</th><td>Included work and explicit boundaries</td><td>Avoid unlimited revisions</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Schedule</th><td>Milestones and client inputs</td><td>Identify dependencies and decision dates</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Price</th><td>Fee, assumptions and applicable tax treatment</td><td>Reconcile included and optional amounts</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Payment</th><td>Amounts, milestones and due-date basis</td><td>Match contract and invoice terms</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Acceptance</th><td>Authorized parties, scope approval and date</td><td>Obtain suitable engagement review</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A proposal can answer a request for proposals or start a conversation with a potential buyer. In either case, adapt it to the decision the client must make. A business plan explains the venture to owners or funders; a client proposal offers a defined engagement. Do not substitute a generic company history for an answer to the client’s requirement.</p><h2 id=\"cost\">Check a fictional fixed-fee project before quoting</h2><p>A catalog photography proposal includes 20 finished images, one agreed background treatment and one consolidated correction round within the stated scope. The seller estimates 20 production hours plus ten planning, communication and editing hours. At an internal loaded rate of $40, included labor is $1,200. Add $300 studio and materials and $150 travel for a defined delivery cost of $1,650.</p><p>At a $2,400 fee, contribution after those included costs is $750, or 31.25% of the fee. Markup on that same $1,650 cost is 45.45%. The percentages use different denominators. Neither establishes net company profit because shared overhead and other omitted costs still need coverage. The $40 rate must represent the chosen cost basis consistently.</p><h2 id=\"scope\">Show how one scope change affects the quote</h2><p>Ten extra hours at the same internal rate add $400. If the fee stays $2,400, included delivery cost becomes $2,050 and contribution falls to $350, or 14.58%. If the client also needs new props, travel or a new deadline, the cost change may be larger. An undefined revision promise makes the original estimate unreliable.</p><p>State which customer inputs are required, how feedback is consolidated, what counts as an accepted output and how requested changes are evaluated. Record the proposed impact on scope, delivery dates and fee before commencing additional work. This teaching template does not establish enforceable contract terms for every jurisdiction.</p><h2 id=\"payment\">Connect milestones to actual cash requirements</h2><p>An illustrative 40% / 40% / 20% payment schedule splits the $2,400 fee into $960, $960 and $480. The schedule must be negotiated, documented and matched to the work and invoice dates. A milestone described in a proposal is not a cleared payment. Test the cash schedule if the second installment arrives late.</p><p>Do not promise the client an unsupported revenue increase to justify the price. Use measurable deliverable acceptance or explicitly labeled scenarios instead. Taxes, intellectual property, confidentiality, cancellation and liability depend on the actual engagement; resolve those terms with suitable review rather than treating this example as a complete legal agreement.</p><h2 id=\"template\">Complete and review the proposal before sending</h2><ol><li>Replace the sample client, objective, deliverables and assumptions.</li><li>Recompute all hours, costs, optional items and the total fee.</li><li>Check the planned completion date against practical capacity and client dependencies.</li><li>Reconcile milestone amounts to the total and record the due-date basis.</li><li>Review commercial and legal terms for the actual engagement and retain the accepted version.</li></ol><p>Use the downloadable worksheet as an editable proposal record, including blank acceptance fields. The <a href=\"/blogs/customer-profitability-analysis/\">customer profitability guide</a> helps evaluate account support beyond the initial job, while the <a href=\"/blogs/calculate-contribution-margin/\">contribution-margin method</a> checks the chosen delivery-cost boundary. Use the <a href=\"/calculators/cash-flow-forecast-template/\">cash forecast</a> to test payment timing.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Download the editable decision record</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/business-proposal-template-worksheet.html\" download>Download the printable HTML worksheet</a>. It includes the completed case and blank fields for your evidence, assumptions, dates and decision. It does not calculate automatically. Edits stay in the open browser until you print or save your completed copy; keep your original separately.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and method</h2><p>The linked sources support the general framework. The calculations, operating constraints and decision examples here are original MyBreakEven teaching material.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.salesforce.com/small-business/how-to-write-a-business-proposal/\">Salesforce: client proposal structure and distinction from a business plan</a></li></ul>",
