    "html": "<p><strong>A useful business goal names an outcome, its baseline, a measure, an owner and a review date. Translate the target into customer activity, delivery capacity and cash requirements before treating it as an achievable commitment.</strong></p><p>All business names, amounts and operating results in the worked examples are fictional teaching assumptions in USD. They are not industry averages, actual customer results or forecasts for your business.</p><h2 id=\"distinction\">Separate a goal, a forecast and an action</h2><p>A goal is an intended result. A forecast is the current evidence-based expectation. An action is something the team will do to influence the result. “Reach $24,000 monthly revenue” is a goal; “current customer evidence supports $21,000” is a forecast; “test one referral offer by the end of the month” is an action. Keep those statements separate so an ambitious goal does not become an unsupported forecast.</p><p>The SMART framework helps make objectives specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound. It does not independently verify demand, resource availability or cause and effect. Write the baseline and source beside the target, including the reporting period and cost or revenue boundary.</p><h2 id=\"drivers\">Translate a fictional revenue target into work</h2><p>A service business currently completes 80 jobs per month at $250: revenue is $20,000. Its goal is $24,000 at the same price within the chosen three-month planning period. That requires 96 jobs, an increase of 16, or 20%. At three worker-hours per job, required delivery time rises from 240 to 288 hours.</p><p>Practical available delivery capacity is 270 hours. The target therefore needs 18 additional hours, a valid scope change or a different price/volume mix. A goal can be clear and measurable while still exceeding capacity. Confirm whether the 270 hours already excludes general administration, absence, travel or rework; do not subtract those twice.</p><h2 id=\"cash\">Check contribution and the payment cycle</h2><p>Assume the defined relevant delivery cost is $100 per job. Each job contributes $150 before period overhead and other omitted costs. Sixteen extra jobs add $2,400 contribution if price and cost remain unchanged. That amount is not an approved hiring budget unless the added-capacity plan, overhead effect and owner requirements have been tested.</p><p>If those extra jobs require $1,600 delivery payments before customer collections, the growth plan needs a dated cash bridge. A $400 referral test paid in advance increases the illustrative pre-collection outlay to $2,000. Avoid subtracting the $1,600 delivery cost twice from the projected result: it is already included in contribution. Cash timing and profit use the same underlying events in different views.</p><h2 id=\"scorecard\">Give each goal a measure, owner and guardrail</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Original example goal record; targets are management choices</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Field</th><th scope=\"col\">Example</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence / control</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Outcome</th><td>96 completed jobs per month by review date</td><td>Accepted job and completion records</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Baseline</th><td>80 jobs at 250</td><td>Same period and revenue basis</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Driver</th><td>Qualified inquiries, acceptance and repeat work</td><td>Track distinct customers and denominators</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Capacity</th><td>288 required hours vs 270 available</td><td>Resolve 18-hour shortfall</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Cash</th><td>2000 illustrative pre-collection outlay</td><td>Confirm dates and available funds</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Owner / review</th><td>Operations owner; weekly driver review</td><td>Monthly outcome and quality review</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Guardrail</th><td>No unapproved scope or quality decline</td><td>Rework, complaints and actual hours</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Use a small set of leading measures the team can act on and lagging measures that show the outcome. Calls made alone may rise without useful orders; completed sales alone may hide refunds or unpaid balances. Choose denominators carefully and avoid changing the definition after the target is missed.</p><h2 id=\"review\">Review progress without rewriting the original target</h2><p>Preserve the approved baseline, goal and forecast. At review, explain differences in volume, price, time, contribution and collection dates. Record whether a missed result reflects demand, delivery limits, changed assumptions or execution. Revise the next forecast using new evidence while retaining accountability for the original goal.</p><p>Use the <a href=\"/blogs/sales-forecast-small-business/\">sales-forecast method</a> for expected demand, <a href=\"/blogs/rolling-forecast/\">rolling forecasting</a> for refreshed assumptions and the <a href=\"/calculators/cash-flow-forecast-template/\">cash forecast</a> for payment dates. Put the corrective action, responsible person, deadline and next evidence check next to the goal rather than in a separate forgotten task list.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Download the editable decision record</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/business-goals-worksheet.html\" download>Download the printable HTML worksheet</a>. It includes the completed case and blank fields for your evidence, assumptions, dates and decision. It does not calculate automatically. Edits stay in the open browser until you print or save your completed copy; keep your original separately.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and method</h2><p>The linked sources support the general framework. The calculations, operating constraints and decision examples here are original MyBreakEven teaching material.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.bdc.ca/en/articles-tools/entrepreneur-toolkit/templates-business-guides/glossary/smart-objectives\">BDC: SMART objectives and resource alignment</a></li></ul>",
