    "image": "/images/title-covers/day2-small-business-strategic-planning-20261104.webp",
    "alt": "Service business founder and colleagues comparing two neighborhood route options.",
    "html": "<p><strong>Small-business strategic planning chooses which customers to serve, how the business will compete and where limited resources will go. A workable strategy makes trade-offs explicit, funds a few coherent priorities and defines how evidence will trigger a review.</strong></p><p>All business names, amounts and operating results in the worked examples are fictional teaching assumptions in USD. They are not industry averages, actual customer results or forecasts for your business.</p><h2 id=\"diagnosis\">Start with the decision the business needs to make</h2><p>Review the actual customer mix, contribution, delivery process, cash cycle and bottleneck. Separate recurring strengths from a one-time favorable month. Use customer evidence and relevant alternatives to identify where the business has a defensible advantage, rather than filling a long SWOT list with untested claims.</p><p>State the central choice in one sentence. For a fictional service company, the choice is whether to concentrate on nearby repeat office customers or pursue a wider premium project market. Both can sound attractive. The strategy must explain which one receives scarce management attention and practical delivery hours first.</p><h2 id=\"options\">Compare choices using the same scarce resource</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Illustrative monthly steady-state cases, before separate setup cash</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Choice</th><th scope=\"col\">Nearby repeat offices</th><th scope=\"col\">Wider premium projects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Customer volume</th><td>20 accounts</td><td>12 projects</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Revenue per customer</th><td>$500</td><td>$900</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Relevant delivery cost per customer</th><td>$250</td><td>$450</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Total revenue</th><td>$10,000</td><td>$10,800</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Contribution</th><td>$5,000</td><td>$5,400</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Delivery hours per customer</th><td>5</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Total delivery hours</th><td>100</td><td>120</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Contribution per delivery hour</th><td>$50</td><td>$45</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Defined common overhead</th><td>$3,000</td><td>$3,000</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Illustrative operating result</th><td>$2,000</td><td>$2,400</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>At 120 practical delivery hours, the premium-project option fully uses the stated capacity; the repeat-office option uses 100 hours. Premium projects produce $400 more monthly result under the assumptions, but lower contribution per delivery hour and no spare delivery capacity. Those facts do not automatically choose the winner: customer evidence, volatility, sales effort and execution risk remain material.</p><h2 id=\"tradeoff\">State what the business will decline or defer</h2><p>If the company chooses nearby repeat offices, it might prioritize route density, consistent scope and renewal discipline, while deferring distant custom work. That choice needs evidence that the nearby market can support the volume and accepted price. The 20 unallocated hours may provide resilience or future capacity; they do not become revenue without customers.</p><p>If it chooses premium projects, it must support the longer delivery scope and potentially different sales and collection cycle. In a separate startup scenario, the repeat-office approach needs $2,000 setup cash and premium projects need $6,000. Those are original assumptions to test, not deductions from the steady-state result. Map the actual cash timing and avoid judging the choice on one monthly profit number alone.</p><h2 id=\"plan\">Fund a short list of connected initiatives</h2><p>For the repeat-office choice, coherent initiatives could include validating a compact service area, standardizing the service checklist and measuring renewals. Allocate hours, budget and an owner to each. Do not simultaneously approve a distant custom-sales push if it consumes the resources the chosen strategy needs.</p><p>Write a one-page action record: choice, evidence, initiative, resources, owner, deadline, outcome measure and review trigger. Connect the operating assumptions to the budget and cash forecast. A list of activities without a resource limit is not an allocation decision. Hiring, leases and other harder-to-reverse commitments need their own explicit decision case.</p><h2 id=\"quarter\">Use a quarterly review to challenge the choice</h2><p>At the chosen review date, examine whether the customer and delivery assumptions held. Track contribution per constrained hour, customer retention, actual selling effort, collection delays and service quality. Compare matching periods and definitions. A missed target may need a tactical adjustment, a revised assumption or a different strategic choice; identify which before adding more initiatives.</p><p>The <a href=\"/resources/business-plan-template/\">business plan</a> explains the wider venture, <a href=\"/blogs/rolling-forecast/\">rolling forecasting</a> refreshes operating expectations and <a href=\"/blogs/customer-profitability-analysis/\">customer profitability analysis</a> tests cost to serve. Preserve the original strategy and review evidence so the next quarter’s changes are deliberate rather than reactions to whichever new opportunity appears first.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Download the editable decision record</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/small-business-strategic-planning-worksheet.html\" download>Download the printable HTML worksheet</a>. It includes the completed case and blank fields for your evidence, assumptions, dates and decision. It does not calculate automatically. Edits stay in the open browser until you print or save your completed copy; keep your original separately.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and method</h2><p>The linked sources support the general framework. The calculations, operating constraints and decision examples here are original MyBreakEven teaching material.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.bdc.ca/en/articles-tools/business-strategy-planning/define-strategy/how-write-strategic-plan\">BDC: strategic choices, priorities and action planning</a></li></ul>",
